{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the AC5 arm-actor classifier fails open when the machineAuthor allowlist is unconfigured**

```js
var logins = machineAuthorModule.machineAuthorLogins(
    Array.isArray(machineLogins) ? machineLogins.join(',') : machineLogins);
if (!logins.length) return 'human';   // ← ANY actor with a login reads as human
```

`machineAuthor` unconfigured → empty allowlist → every labeled-event actor (including any bot with write access) classifies as `'human'` and **arms the transplant**. Combined with `query.notMachine` — which is documented as inert when no machineAuthor is configured (`js/sm/sources/githubSource.js:1114`) — a deployment that sets `smGuestTransplant` but not `machineAuthor` has **no machine-actor check at all** on the one gate whose docblock calls the label "the voucher for executing foreign code in our CI".

I understand this is deliberate (test-pinned at `test_guestTransplant.js:705`) and consistent with the machineAuthor degradation doctrine for *review/rework* gates. But those gates degrade to "no auto action" on empty allowlists, while this one degrades to "**auto-execute foreign code**" — the opposite direction. Please confirm the intent; if confirmed, this can stay as-is (downgrading to a docs nit). If not, an empty allowlist should return `'unverified'` here — the one gate that arms untrusted code should be the fail-closed one, matching the module's own header ("missing proof refuses — fail closed").
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `list[0]` arbitrarily picks which PR's state decides the GC**

```js
if (Array.isArray(list) && list.length) prState = String(list[0].state || '').toUpperCase();
```

The query is `state=all`, so if more than one PR ever shares the `ai/guest-N` head (a stale closed machine PR plus a human/automation-opened PR from the same branch), an arbitrary entry — not necessarily the OPEN one — decides whether the branch is deleted. A closed `list[0]` beside a live OPEN machine PR GCs a branch that still carries an open transplant. Prefer scanning for an `OPEN` entry first and only falling back to the first closed one.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment6.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the precedence comment contradicts both the code and the machineSm precedent it cites**

```js
// The sm-style knob: inert (null) unless the project configures the
// section — jobParams first, then the project config (same
// override-before-default priority every machineSm knob uses). An
```

The comment says "jobParams first, then the project config", but the code resolves `projectConfig.smGuestTransplant` first:

```js
var section = (projectConfig && projectConfig.smGuestTransplant) ||
    (jobParams && jobParams.smGuestTransplant) || null;
```

That matches the actual machineSm pattern — `resolveMachineSmConfig` (js/machineSmAgent.js:342) reads `projectConfig.machineSm` (override) before jobParams — so the **code is right and the comment is backwards**. Worth fixing: an operator reading the comment would tune `jobParams.smGuestTransplant` and be silently shadowed by a project-config section. (The unit test only covers the no-conflict case — "jobParams wins when the project config carries none" — so nothing pins either precedence for the both-set case.)
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment7.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `guestExec`'s `ok: true` field is dead**

```js
function guestExec(repoInfo, command) {
    var res = cli_execute_command({ command: command });
    var out = String((res && (res.output || res.stdout)) || res || '');
    var trimmed = out.trim();
    return { ok: true, out: trimmed };
}
```

`cli_execute_command` signals failure by throwing, so `ok` is always `true` and no caller reads it — every call site either uses `.out` or lets the throw propagate (which is the intended behavior). Either drop the field, or actually catch and return `{ ok: false, error }` so callers can distinguish "command ran, empty output" from "transport failed" — today `guestBranchExists` has to re-implement that distinction with its own try/catch.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment9.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: unrelated encoding churn in two rule descriptions**

This hunk (and the `dev-on-assignment` description at line 556) rewrites the rule descriptions from `\u2014` JSON escapes to literal `—` characters. After `JSON.parse` the strings are **byte-identical** — no behavior change, but ~2 KB of diff noise inside the exact rules this PR's reviewers need to diff carefully (the conflict-rework description sits directly under the new guest-transplant rule). Reverting the two description lines to their original spelling keeps the diff scoped to gh-1140.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment10.md"}

